{"p":"/chat/cross-server-chat","t":"Cross-server chat","d":"Cross-server chat allows players to interact with others across different servers.","h":"<p><strong>Cross-server chat</strong> is a native extension of <a class=\"ref\" href=\"#/class/TextChatService\"><code>TextChatService</code></a> that allows players to safely communicate with others across different servers in the same universe. This increases social engagement, especially in games with low server occupancy or during live events with a global community.</p>\n<p>Roblox automatically groups servers based on age, language, and occupancy to ensure meaningful and safe interactions. Like default Roblox chat, cross-server chat includes full support for abuse reporting, text filtering, chat rules, muting, and blocking.</p>\n<div class=\"callout c-info\">\n<p>Each global chat channel connects about 200 users to ensure chat remains readable and performant. If users send messages too quickly, a throttle applies to the senders and receivers, and messages drop to protect the chat system.</p>\n</div>\n<h2>Enable cross-server chat</h2>\n<p>When you enable cross-server chat, the <a href=\"#/docs/chat/chat-window\">chat window</a> includes two tabs for either single or multi-server communication:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Here</strong> - Players can chat with others in the same server.</li>\n<li><strong>Global</strong> - Players can chat with others across different servers.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><img loading=\"lazy\" src=\"data:image/webp;base64,\" alt=\"Core components of the text chat window with cross-server chat enabled.\" /></p>\n<p>Cross-server chat is <strong>enabled by default</strong> for all games, but you have full control to enable or disable it according to your game's design requirements. To access the cross-server chat setting:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Navigate to the <a href=\"https://create.roblox.com/dashboard/creations\">Creator Dashboard</a>.</li>\n<li>Select a game, then in the left-hand navigation, navigate to <strong>Audience</strong> &gt; <strong>Communication Settings</strong>.</li>\n<li>Under the <strong>Cross-server chat</strong> section, toggle <strong>Allow cross-server chat</strong> on or off to enable or disable cross-server chat.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"callout c-warning\">\n<p>Even if cross-server chat is enabled, the <strong>Global</strong> tab is disabled in <a href=\"https://create.roblox.com/docs/production/monetization/private-servers\">private servers</a> to respect the privacy of users who only want to access your game with specific friends.</p>\n</div>\n<h2>Global chat commands</h2>\n<p>The <strong>Global</strong> tab supports a subset of standard chat commands for global chat:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>/mute &lt;username&gt;</code> - Mutes a specific user in global chat.</li>\n<li><code>/unmute &lt;username&gt;</code> - Unmutes a user.</li>\n<li><code>/help</code> - Lists commands available within global chat.</li>\n<li><code>/clear</code> - Clears the local chat window history.</li>\n</ul>","kid":{"simple":"Cross-server chat lets players talk with people in other servers of the same game, not just the server they are in. It is great when your game has only a few players per server, or during a big live event. Roblox picks which servers get grouped together and keeps the same safety tools as normal chat. It is turned on by default, and you can switch it off on the Creator Dashboard.","analogy":"It is like a school with many classrooms. The Here tab is talking to kids in your own classroom, and the Global tab is the shared hallway where kids from lots of classrooms can chat.","keyPoints":["Cross-server chat is part of `TextChatService` and connects players across different servers of the same game.","When it is on, the chat window shows two tabs: Here (your server) and Global (other servers too).","Roblox groups servers by age, language, and how full they are, so chats are safe and make sense.","Each global channel connects about 200 users so it stays readable. If people send messages too fast, some messages get dropped.","Reporting, text filtering, chat rules, muting, and blocking all still work in global chat.","It is on by default. To change it: Creator Dashboard, pick your game, Audience > Communication Settings, then toggle Allow cross-server chat.","The Global tab never shows in private servers, even if the setting is on.","The Global tab supports a few commands: `/mute <username>`, `/unmute <username>`, `/help`, and `/clear`."],"terms":{"server":"One running copy of your game. A popular game might have 100 servers, each with its own group of players.","universe":"Your whole game, including all its places. Cross-server chat only connects servers from the same universe.","occupancy":"How many players are in a server right now. A server for 20 players with 3 people in it has low occupancy.","throttle":"A speed limit. If people send messages too fast, Roblox slows things down and drops some messages to protect chat.","channel":"A chat room inside the chat system. A global channel holds about 200 users from many servers.","private server":"A server that only invited people can join. The Global tab is turned off there to respect their privacy."},"watchOut":["Expecting Global to work in a private server. It is always hidden there, so test it in a normal public server.","Looking for the setting in Studio. The cross-server chat toggle lives on the Creator Dashboard under Audience > Communication Settings.","Thinking `/clear` deletes messages for everyone. It only clears the chat history in your own chat window."]}}